     1	\section{Introduction}
     2	Reconfiguration problems have recently emerged in different fields of computer science, such as satisfiability~\cite{Gopalan, cnf_reconf},
     3	constraint satisfaction~\cite{constraint_sat1, constraint_sat2}, computational geometry~\cite{geometry1, geometry2}, and quantum complexity theory~\cite{quantum}, even though reconfiguration questions have been posed in mathematics for well over a century~\cite{15_puzzle}.
     4	More practically, in real world problems, it is often the case that the systems we work with have a preferred state or one of optimal performance, but currently find themselves out of such a configuration.
     5	It is natural to try to answer questions such as: \textit{can we bring our system to the desired state without breaking it?} or \textit{what is the minimum number of tweaks we have to perform to do so?}
     6	
     7	Not surprisingly, reconfiguration has found its way into graph theory, with work on the reconfiguration of classical graph structures recently appearing in the literature.
     8	While \pname{Independent Set Reconfiguration} is by far the most well studied graph theoretical reconfiguration problem, with a corpus of at least a dozen papers~\cite{Kaminski,sliding_tokens,isr_cograph1,isr_cograph2,isr_claw_free,sliding_pspace_completeness,param_tj,param_reconf,td_reconf,bandwidth_reconf,Lokshtanov,Ito1}, 
     9	many others have also attracted the attention of the community, such as \pname{Clique Reconfiguration}~\cite{Ito1, Ito2} and \pname{Vertex Coloring Reconfiguration}~\cite{Cereceda,chordal_color_reconf,coloring_reconf_pspace,td_reconf}.
    10	Many of these works formalize their problems through the \textit{reconfiguration framework}.
    11	Under this framework, a set of tokens is placed on some vertices of the input graph and is said to be a \textit{state} if it satisfies the desired property, e.g. induces an independent set.
    12	Two states are \textit{adjacent} if we can apply an atomic operation to one in order to obtain the other, e.g. add a token to a vertex.
    13	Three operations have been given considerable attention in the literature:
    14	
    15	\begin{itemize}
    16	    \item \textbf{Token Sliding (TS)}: Move the token on a vertex $v$ to any neighbor of $v$. 
    17	    \item \textbf{Token Jumping (TJ)}: Move one of the tokens to any other vertex of the graph.
    18	    \item \textbf{Token Addition/Removal (TAR)}: Add a token to any vertex of the graph, or remove a token from any vertex.
    19	\end{itemize}
    20	
    21	Under both TJ and TS, the cardinality of the token set remains unchanged throughout the whole reconfiguration process.
    22	This is clearly not the case with TAR and the rule as is allows for trivial solutions to reconfiguration in many cases.
    23	For instance, on \pname{Independent Set Reconfiguration} under TAR, we could remove one vertex of the initial set at a time until we have an empty independent set, then add each vertex of the target set, completing the reconfiguration.
    24	To keep the question interesting and avoid this triviality phenomenon, a lower or upper bound is imposed on the cardinality of the token set; in our example, we would add the restriction that the intermediate independent sets must have \textit{at least} $k$ vertices.
    25	Since the direction of the bound is usually clear by the problem definition, the bounded version of TAR is usually referred to as $k$\textit{-TAR}.
    26	Throughout our text, unless the bound of $k$-TAR is relevant to the discussion, in an abuse of terminology, we use simply TAR.
    27	
    28	Given a graph $G$, a property $\pi$, an operation $P$, we define the \textit{reconfiguration graph} $R_G(\pi, P)$, which has as vertex set the states defined by $\pi$ and an edge between two states if they are adjacent under $P$.
    29	Under this formalism, we say that $A$ can be reconfigured into $B$ if the vertices corresponding to $A$ and $B$ in $R_G(\pi, P)$ are in the same connected component.
    30	When discussing the complexity of a reconfiguration problem, the natural certificate is the sequence of states used to reconfigure $A$ into $B$.
    31	This sequence, however, is not necessarily of polynomial size.
    32	Thus, to show that a reconfiguration problem is \NPc, we must either use another certificate that can be verified in polynomial time, or we must prove that \YES\ instances have a reconfiguration sequence of polynomial length.
    33	For an example of the latter, we refer to the work of \citeauthor{Lokshtanov}~\cite{Lokshtanov} on \pname{Vertex Cover Reconfiguration}, more specifically their result on bipartite graphs under TAR.
    34	
    35	In this work, we explore the reconfiguration problem of another fundamental structure of graph theory: \textit{vertex separators}; in this version of the problem, vertices $s,t$ we wish to separate are fixed, i.e. a set $S \subseteq V(G)$ satisfies the property of interest $\pi$ if and only if, in $G - S$, $s$ and $t$ are disconnected.
    36	We refer to this problem as \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration}.
    37	We begin by showing that, aside from a set of easily identifiable negative instances.
    38	In terms of hardness results, we show that, on a subclass of bipartite graphs, the problem is \PSPACE-\Complete\ under TS and \NPc\ under TAR/TJ.
    39	On the positive side, we first show that, if a graph class  is tame, i.e. has a polynomially bounded number of minimal separators, under TAR/TJ, \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} can be solved in polynomial time.
    40	Then, we turn our attention to classes that do not satisfy this condition.
    41	In particular, we present a characterization for $\{3P_1,\text{diamond}\}$-free graphs, which we use to show that reconfiguration is always possible under TAR/TJ and easily verifiable under TS.
    42	Our final results concern the class of $2$-connected series-parallel graphs, to which we provide a thorough analysis of the possible $st$-separators, which we use to show that \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} is also always positive under TAR/TJ, leaving the question under TS as future work.
    43	Other questions we leave unanswered include the study of other non-tame classes, particularly the ones described by \citeauthor{Milanic}~\cite{Milanic}, and the search for reasonable sufficient conditions for the existence of polynomial time algorithms under TS.
FILE src/sections/prelim.tex
     1	\section{Preliminaries}
     2	
     3	In this work we deal with finite simple graphs, and follow the terminology of standard textbooks in the area, such as~\cite{murty}. 
     4	Given a connected graph $G$, we denote by $\diam(G)$ the length of the longest shortest path between any two vertices of $G$.
     5	Given two non-adjacent vertices $s,t \in V(G)$, we say that $S \subseteq V(G) \setminus \{s,t\}$ is an $st$-separator if, on the subgraph $G - S$ induced by $V(G) \setminus S$, there is no path between $s$ and $t$; $S$ is minimal if no proper subset of $S$ is also an $st$-separator.
     6	We say that a sequence $ \mathcal{S} = \langle S_1, \dots, S_n \rangle$ of subsets of $V(G)$ is a \textit{reconfiguration sequence} if, for all $i$, $S_i$ is an $st$-separator.
     7	$\mathcal{S}$ is a \textit{reconfiguration sequence under $P$} if, for all $i \in [n-1]$, we can obtain $S_{i+1}$ by applying operation $P$ to $S_i$ once.
     8	For example, if our operation of interest is token sliding, we must replace one of the vertices in $S_i$ by one of its neighbors.
     9	Formally, for our three rules of interest, we say that two separators $S_i$, $S_j$ are \textit{adjacent}, denoted by $S_i \leftrightarrow S_j$, if the following holds:
    10	
    11	\begin{itemize}
    12		\item[\textbf{TS}] $S_i \leftrightarrow S_j$ if  $| S_i | =| S_j |$, $ S_i  \setminus S_j =\{u_i\}$, $ S_j  \setminus S_i=\{v_j\}$ and $u_iv_j \in E(G)$.
    13		
    14		\item[\textbf{TJ}] $S_i \leftrightarrow S_j$ if  $| S_i | = | S_j |$ and $| S_i  \setminus S_j |= | S_j  \setminus S_i|=1$.
    15		
    16		\item[\textbf{$k$-TAR}]$S_i \leftrightarrow S_j$ if  $| S_i \Delta S_j |=|( S_i \setminus S_j) \cup ( S_j \setminus S_i)  |=1$ and $\max(|S_i|, |S_j|) \leq k$.
    17	\end{itemize}
    18	
    19	When describing our instances, we adopt the convention that $TS(G, S_a, S_b)$ means that we want to reconfigure $S_a$ into $S_b$ on graph $G$ under TS; for $k$-TAR, we use $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k)$.
    20	Following the notation previously used in the literature, if the instance is positive, i.e. $S_a$ can be reconfigured into $S_b$, we use the notation $S_a \leftrightsquigarrow S_b$.
FILE src/sections/tar-tj-eq.tex
     1	\section{TAR-TJ equivalence}
     2	
     3	During their study of \pname{Clique Reconfiguration}, \citeauthor{Ito2}~\cite{Ito2} proved that rules TAR, TJ
     4	and TS are equivalent in terms of the complexity of the problem and that, in fact, the length of the reconfiguration sequences are within a factor of two from each other.
     5	Inspired by their work, our first result states that \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} is equivalent under TAR and TJ, in the sense that, given a graph $G$ and two vertex separators  $S_a,S_b$ in $G$,  if we have a $TJ$-instance $(G,S_a,S_b)$  we can construct a $TAR-$instance such that $TJ(G,S_a,S_b)=TAR(S_a',S_b',k)$ and if we have  a $TAR$-instance we can construct a $TJ-$instance such that $TAR(G,S_a,S_b, K)=TJ(S_a',S_b')$.
     6	Specifically, we show that every TJ instance has a corresponding TAR instance and, in the other direction, either the TAR instance is trivially negative or there is some TJ instance to which it is equivalent to.
     7	In the following lemma, we show that, if $S_a, S_b$ are two minimal $uv$-separators such that $\vert S_a \vert = \vert S_b \vert$ and $S_a \leftrightsquigarrow S_b$ under $TAR$, there exists a shortest $TAR$ sequence that can be constructed in linear time adding and removing a vertex in each step. 
     8	
     9	\begin{lemma}
    10	    \label{lem:canon_tar}
    11		Let $(G, S_a, S_b, k+1)$ be a $TAR$-instance of \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration}, where $S_a, S_b$ are a pair of $uv$-separators satisfying $|S_a| = |S_b| = k$.
    12		If $S_a \leftrightsquigarrow S_b$, there exists a shortest reconfiguration sequence $\langle S_a = S_1,..., S_\ell = S_b \rangle$ such that $|S_i| \geq k$ for all $i \in [\ell]$, with equality holding if and only if $i$ is odd.
    13	\end{lemma}
    14	
    15	\begin{proof}
    16	    Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a shortest reconfiguration sequence for $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k+1)$ of length $\ell$.
    17	    We modify $\mathcal{S}$ by the following algorithm that, at each step, selects exactly one state of the sequence and increases its size by two, while $\min \{|S_i| \mid i \in [\ell]\} < k$.
    18	    If the algorithm is not done, take $j$ as the smallest index such that $|S_j| = \min \{|S_i| \mid i \in [\ell]\}$.
    19	    Since $j \notin \{1, \ell\}$, $S_j = S_{j-1} \setminus \{a\}$ and  $S_j = S_{j+1} \setminus \{b\}$, and $a \neq b$ because $\mathcal{S}$ is a shortest sequence.
    20	    Furthermore, it holds that $|S_j \cup \{a,b\}| \leq k+1$ and we can construct another sequence $\mathcal{S}' = \angled{S_1, \dots, S_{j-1}, S_j \cup \{a,b\}, S_{j+1}, \dots S_\ell}$ and set $\mathcal{S} \gets \mathcal{S}'$.
    21	    When the algorithm stops, every $S_i$ satisfies $|S_i| \in \{k, k+1\}$ and, since it never modifies $S_1$ nor $S_\ell$ and every operation modifies the size of the state it acts upon, $|S_2| = k+1$; a direct inductive argument shows that $|S_{2i}| = k+1$ for all $i \in [\ell/2]$.
    22	\end{proof}
    23	
    24	In the following, let $\dist_{X}(G, S_a, S_b)$ be the size of a shortes reconfiguration sequence from $S_a$ to $S_b$ according to rule $X$.
    25	
    26	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:tj_tar}
    27		Let $G$ be a graph and let $S_a, S_b$ be any pair of $uv$-separators in $G$ such that $|S_a| = |S_b| = k$.
    28		It holds $TJ(G, S_a, S_b) = TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k+1)$ and $\dist_{TJ}(G, S_a, S_b) = \dist_{(k+1)-TAR}(G, S_a,S_b)/2$.
    29	\end{lemma}
    30	
    31	\begin{proof}
    32	    If $\mathcal{S}_{TJ} = \angled{S_1 = S_a, \dots, S_\ell = S_b}$ is a shortest TJ-reconfiguration sequence, we construct the sequence $\mathcal{Q}_{TAR} = \angled{Q_1, \dots, Q_r}$ such that $Q_{2i-1} \gets S_i$ for all $i \in [\ell]$ and, for all even $j \in [2\ell - 1]$, $Q_j = Q_{j-1} \cup Q_{j+1} = S_{j/2} \cup S_{j/2 + 1}$.
    33	    Note that, in the latter case, $|Q_j| \leq k+1$, since $S_{j/2}$ and $S_{j/2 + 1}$ are consecutive states of $\mathcal{S}_{TJ}$; furthermore, $Q_j \setminus Q_{j+1}$ is empty if $j$ is even or has cardinality equal to one if $j$ is odd.
    34	    As such, we conclude $\mathcal{Q}_{TAR}$ is a certificate for $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k+1)$ and that $\dist_{(k+1)-TAR}(G, S_a, S_b) \leq 2\dist_{TJ}(G, S_a, S_b)$.
    35	    
    36	    For the converse, let $\mathcal{S}_{TAR} = \angled{S_1 = S_a, \dots, S_\ell = S_b}$ be a shortest $(k+1)$-TAR-reconfiguration sequence satisfying Lemma~\ref{lem:canon_tar}.
    37	    We show that the sequence $\mathcal{Q}_{TJ} = \angled{Q_1, \dots, Q_r}$ where $Q_{j} \gets S_{2j-1}$, for all $j$ satisfying $1 \leq 2j -1 \leq \ell$, is a TJ-reconfiguration sequence.
    38	    By Lemma~\ref{lem:canon_tar}, it holds that $|Q_j| = k$ and $Q_{j} \triangle Q_{j+1} = S_{2j-1} \triangle S_{2j+1} = \{a,b\}$ and the jump operation is executed in such a way that, say, $a$ is replaced by $b$.
    39	    Moreover, $\dist_{TJ}(G, S_a, S_b) \leq \dist_{(k+1)-TAR}(G, S_a, S_b)/2$, concluding the proof.
    40	\end{proof}
    41	
    42	Observation~\ref{obs:tar_no} states that the value of $k+1$ imposed by the hypothesis of Lemma~\ref{lem:tj_tar} is optimal, i.e. there are instances $TJ(G, S_a, S_b) = \YES$ where at least one of the endpoints of the reconfiguration is a minimal separator but $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k) = \NOi$.
    43	Furthermore, it prunes trivially negative cases which, as we show further below, are the only ones that may not have a corresponding TJ instance.
    44	
    45	\begin{observation}
    46	    \label{obs:tar_no}
    47		Let $TAR(G, S_a,S_b,k)$ be an instance of \pname{Vertex Separator reconfiguration} such that $S_a \neq S_b$. If at least one of $S_a, S_b$ is minimal and has exactly $k$ vertices, then $TAR(G, S_a,S_b,k)= \NOi$.
    48	\end{observation}
    49	
    50	\begin{lemma}
    51	    \label{lem:tar_tj}
    52	    Let $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k)$ be an instance of \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} that does not satisfy Observation~\ref{obs:tar_no}, $S_a'$ be a set of cardinality $k-1$ such that $S_a' \cap S_a$ contains a $uv$-separator and $S_b'$ be defined analogously.
    53	    It holds that $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k) = TAR(G, S_a', S_b', k) = TJ(G, S_a', S_b')$ and $\dist_{TJ}(G, S_a', S_b') = \dist_{k-TAR}(G, S_a',S_b')/2$.
    54	\end{lemma}
    55	
    56	\begin{proof}
    57	    To see that the first equality holds, note that $S_a$ ($S_b$) can be easily reconfigured into $S_a'$ ($S_b'$), since both $S_a$ ($S_b$) and $S_a'$ ($S_b'$) contain the \textit{same} $uv$-separator of $G$.
    58	    For the second, note that the triple $(S_a', S_b', k)$ satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:tj_tar} precisely because neither $S_a$ nor $S_b$ are minimal and of size $k$.
    59	\end{proof}
    60	
    61	The direct application of Lemmas~\ref{lem:tj_tar}, \ref{lem:tar_tj}, and Observation~\ref{obs:tar_no} yield our equivalence theorem.
    62	
    63	\begin{theorem}
    64	    \label{thm:tj_tar_eq}
    65		Let $G$ be a graph,  $u,v \in V(G)$, and $S_a, S_b$ two $uv$-separators. The following statements hold:
    66		\begin{itemize}
    67			\item [(i)] If $|S_a| = |S_b| = k$, instance $TJ(G, S_a, S_b)$ has an equivalent TAR-instance $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k+1)$ which can be built in linear time and $\dist_{TJ}(G, S_a, S_b) = \dist_{(k+1)-TAR}(G, S_a,S_b)/2$;
    68			\item[(ii)] If $|S_a| \leq |S_b| = k$ and $S_b$ is minimal, $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k) = \NOi$;
    69			\item[(iii)] Otherwise, instance $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k)$ has an equivalent TJ-instance $TJ(G, S_a', S_b')$ that can be generated in polynomial time satisfying $\dist_{TJ}(G, S_a', S_b') = \dist_{k-TAR}(G, S_a',S_b')/2$.
    70		\end{itemize}
    71	\end{theorem}
    72	
    73	We cannot ascertain whether TS is equivalent or not to TAR/TJ in a similar sense as to the one given in Theorem~\ref{thm:tj_tar_eq}.
    74	It is not surprising, however, that we cannot do so: as we show in Corollary~\ref{cor:completeness} of Theorem~\ref{thm:hardnesses}, if all three rules are equivalent even if only on a very specific subclass of bipartite graphs, we would have that $\NP = \PSPACE$.
    75	Figure~\ref{fig:ts_tar_diff} presents an example where it is not immediate that one cannot reconfigure $S_a = \{u_1, u_2\}$ into $S_b = \{u_7, u_8\}$ under TS, but we can do so under TAR/TJ.
    76	
