     1	\section{Polynomial time results}
     2	
     3	\citeauthor{Milanic}~\cite{Milanic} studied the behavior of the family of minimal vertex separators on graph classes defined by forbidden families of small induced subgraphs.
     4	Using their nomenclature, a graph class $\mathcal{G}$ is \textit{tame} if the family of minimal vertex separators of each $G \in \mathcal{G}$, denoted by $\mathbf{S}$, has size bounded by a polynomial $p_{\mathcal{G}}$ evaluated at $|V(G)|$.
     5	The opening result of this section states that if $G$ belongs to a tame class, then \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} is solvable in polynomial time.
     6	
     7	
     8	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:overlap_graph}
     9	    Let $u,v$ be two vertices of $G$, $\mathbf{S}_{uv}(G)$ be the family of minimal $uv$-separators of $G$, and
    10	    $H_{uv}$ be the graph where $V(H_{uv})= \mathbf{S}_{uv}(G)$ and $E(H_{uv})=\{S_i,S_j \ \ \vert \  \  S_i,S_j \in  \mathbf{S}_{uv}(G) \ \ and \ \ \vert S_i\cup S_j \vert \leq k \}$.
    11	    For any two $uv$-separators $S_a, S_b$ of $G$, $TAR(G, S_a,S_b,k) = \YES$ if and only if there exists a path from $S_a'$ to $S_b'$ in $H_{uv}$, where $S_a'$ and  $S_b'$ are minimal $uv$-separators of $G$ with $S_a' \subseteq S_a$ and $S_b' \subseteq S_b$.
    12	\end{lemma}
    13	
    14	\begin{proof}
    15	    Let  $S_a, S_b$ two $uv$-separators in $G$.
    16	    Suppose that $TAR(G, S_a, S_b, k) = \YES$, let $\langle S_1, \dots, S_r \rangle$ be a reconfiguration sequence between $S_a$ and $S_b$, and let $\langle \mathcal{S}_1, \dots \mathcal{S}_r\rangle$ be a sequence such that $\mathcal{S}_i$ is the family of all minimal $uv$-separators that are subsets of $S_i$.
    17	    Note that $\mathcal{S}_i \cup \mathcal{S}_{i+1}$ is a clique of $H_{uv}$ since, for any $A \in \mathcal{S}_i$ and $B \in \mathcal{S}_{i+1}$, $|A \cup B| \leq |S_i \cup S_{i+1}| \leq k$.
    18	    Thus, there is a path between $S_a'$ and $S_b'$ in $H_{uv}$.
    19	    %{\color {red} muito boa, bem mais elegante} {\color {blue} valeu!}  
    20	    
    21	    For the converse, let $\langle S_a', S_1', \dots, S_r', S_b' \rangle$ be some path between $S_a'$ and $S_b'$ in $H_{uv}$; note that since $|S_i' \cup S_{i+1}'| \leq k$, we can greedily reconfigure $S_i'$ into $S_{i+1}'$ without violating the cardinality constraint; by a straightforward inductive argument, we can reconfigure $S_a'$ into $S_b'$ and, consequently, $S_a \leftrightsquigarrow S_b$.
    22	\end{proof}
    23	
    24	
    25	\begin{theorem} \label{separadorespolinomial}
    26		If $G$ has a polynomially bounded number of minimal vertex separators, then \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} can be solved in polynomial time under TAR/TJ. 
    27	\end{theorem}
    28	
    29	\begin{proof}
    30	    Along with the result of \citeauthor{separator_generation}~\cite{separator_generation} that the family of minimal separators of an $n$-vertex graph $G$ can be generated in $\bigO{|\mathbf{S}_{uv}|n^3}$, Lemma~\ref{lem:overlap_graph} directly implies that it suffices to construct $H_{uv}$ and check if there is some minimal separator contained in $S_a$ in the same connected component of a minimal separator contained in $S_b$.
    31	    Since $H_{uv}$ has a number of vertices polynomial on the size of $G$, this algorithm runs in time polynomial in $n$.
    32	\end{proof}
    33	
    34	
    35	\begin{corollary}
    36		\pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} can be solved in polynomial time for chordal graphs under TAR/TJ.
    37	\end{corollary}
    38	
    39	
    40	\subsection{Non-tame classes}
    41	
    42	Also in~\cite{Milanic}, \citeauthor{Milanic} determined the three families of graphs on at most four vertices that, when forbidden, do not yield a tame class.
    43	Specifically, if $\mathcal{F} \in \{\{3P_1, diamond\}, \{claw, K_4, C_4, diamond\}, \{K_3, C_4\}\}$, then the class of $\mathcal{F}$-free graphs is \textit{not} tame; all other graph classes that exclude graphs on at most four vertices are tame.
    44	In this final section of the paper, we show that not only can we solve \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} in polynomial time on  $\{3P_1, diamond\}$-free graphs and series-parallel graphs, but that reconfiguration is \textit{always} possible under TAR/TJ for these classes.
    45	For the first, we show that, under TS, we can decide in polynomial time whether reconfiguration is possible.
    46	
    47	\subsubsection{$\boldsymbol{\{3P_1, diamond\}}$-free}
    48	
    49	Before dealing with the reconfiguration problem for $\{3P_1, diamond\}$-free graphs, we present a novel characterization of the class that makes the reconfiguration question almost trivial.
    50	 
    51	\begin{theorem}
    52	    \label{thm:char}
    53		Let $G$ be a connected not complete graph with at least four vertices %{\color{red} não é bem isso, certo? }
    54		such that $G$ is not a clique and $G \neq C_5$. 
    55		Then $G$ is $\{3P_1, diamond\}$-free if and only if $\diam(G) \leq 3$ and one of the following statements hold:
    56		
    57		
    58		\begin{itemize}
    59			\item[(i)] $G$ is the union of two cliques $Q_1, Q_2$ and has exactly one cut vertex; or
    60			\item[(ii)] $G$ is the disjoint union of two cliques $Q_1, Q_2$ and the edges between the cliques form a matching.
    61		\end{itemize}
    62	\end{theorem}
    63	
    64	\begin{proof}
    65	    For the forward direction, suppose $G$ has a universal vertex $v$; since $G$ is not a clique, $\diam(G) = 2$, and let $\{u,v,w\}$ be an induced $P_3$ of $G$.
    66	    Since $G$ is $3P_1$-free, any $z \in V(G)$ is either in $N(u)$ or $N(v)$ but, since $G$ is also diamond-free and $v$ is universal, $z$ is not in both, otherwise $\{u,v,w,z\}$ is a diamond; moreover, if there are two non-adjacent vertices $a,b \in N(u)$, $\{a,u,v,b\}$ is an induced diamond, so $N[u]$ and $N[w]$ are cliques.1 Thus, $G$ satisfies condition (i) with $v$ as a cut vertex.
    67	    
    68	    If $G$ does not have a universal vertex, let $u,w \in V(G)$ be a pair of non-adjacent vertices and note that $N(u) \cup N(w) \cup \{u,w\} = V(G)$, otherwise $G$ would not be $3P_1$-free.
    69	    Furthermore, there are no two adjacent vertices in $N(u) \cap N(w)$, otherwise we would have a diamond, $N(u) \setminus N(w)$ is a clique, otherwise $G\left[\{w\} \cup N(u) \setminus N(w)\right]$ would contain $3P_1$ as an induced subgraph, and no vertex in  $N(u) \setminus N(w)$ is adjacent to more than one vertex in $N(w)$, otherwise we would have an induced diamond.
    70	    We branch our analysis on the size of $N(u) \cap N(w)$.
    71	    \begin{enumerate}
    72	        \item If $N(u) \cap N(w) = \emptyset$, no vertex of $N(u)$ has more than one neighbor in $N(w)$, so the edge between $N(u)$ and $N(w)$ form a matching.
    73	        
    74	        \item Suppose that $\{v\} = N(u) \cap N(w)$. 
    75	        If there is some $a \in N(u)$ that $v$ is not adjacent to, $v$ is not adjacent to \textit{any} vertex $b \in N(u)$, otherwise we would have $\{a, u, b, v\}$ as an induced diamond of $G$.
    76	        In turn, $v$ is either adjacent the entirety of $N(u)$ or to no vertex of $N(u)$ (the same holds with respect to $N(w)$).
    77	        However, since $v$ is not universal and w.l.o.g, it is not adjacent to $N(u)$.
    78	        We have two subcases.
    79	        \begin{enumerate}
    80	            \item If $v$ is not adjacent to $N(w)$, each vertex in $N(w)$ must be adjacent to every vertex in $N(u)$, otherwise we would have an induced $3P_1$; however, if $\max\left\{|N(u)|, |N(w)|\right\} \geq 2$, we have an induced diamond, so $G$ must be a $C_5$, a contradiction to our hypothesis.
    81	            \item If $v$ is adjacent to $N(w)$ we are done: $V(G)$ is partitioned into the cliques $N[u], N[w]$ and the edges between them form a matching.
    82	        \end{enumerate}
    83	        \item Finally, if $\{v,v'\} = N(u) \cap N(w)$, we cannot have both $v,v'$ non-adjacent to the same vertex $a \in N(u) \setminus N(w)$, otherwise $\{a,v,v'\}$ form an induced $3P_1$ of $G$, nor have both of them adjacent to $a$, otherwise $\{v, a, u, v'\}$ would form an induced diamond; as such, suppose $v$ is adjacent to every vertex in $N(u) \setminus \{v, v'\}$.
    84	        We again have to branch in two subcases.
    85	        \begin{itemize}
    86	            \item If $v$ is not adjacent to the vertices in $N(w) \setminus \{v,v'\}$, $v'$ must be adjacent to them.
    87	            Therefore, we can partition $G$ into the cliques $(N[u] \setminus v'), (N[w]  \setminus v)$ and the edges between the cliques form a matching, otherwise we would have an induced diamond.
    88	            \item If $v$ is adjacent to $N(w) \setminus \{v, v'\}$, then at least one of $N(w) \setminus \{v, v'\}$, $N(u) \setminus \{v, v'\}$ is empty, otherwise we have either a copy of $3P_1$ that includes $v'$ or an induced diamond $\{u,v,a,b\}$, where $a \in N(u)$ and $b \in N(w)$.
    89	        \end{itemize}
    90	    \end{enumerate}
    91	    
    92	    It is straightforward to verify that $\diam(G) \leq 3$.
    93	    The converse follows directly and we omit it for brevity.
    94	\end{proof}
    95	
    96	The proof of the following theorem relies on the fact that the only minimal separators are either the universal cut vertex or we must choose, for each edge between the cliques, exactly one of its endpoints.
    97	In the first case, the answer for \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} under TAR/TJ is always \YES, while under TS it suffices to check whether the number of tokens on each clique is equal in the initial and final separators; for an example of this case we refer to Figure~\ref{fig:3p1_diamond_case_1}.
    98	For the latter, the case under TAR/TJ boils down to the same analysis.
    99	For TS however, things require a bit more of work: if $u,v$ are the vertices we want to separate and every edge between $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ has an endpoint in $\{u,v\}$, then the analysis is also equivalent to the previous case; if, on the other hand, there is at least one edge that has neither $u$ nor $v$ as an endpoint, we can freely move tokens between $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ through it.
   100	For an example of each of theses cases, we point to Figure~\ref{fig:3p1_diamond_case_2}.
   101	
   102	\begin{theorem}
   103	    Under TAR/TJ and TS, \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} can be solved in polynomial time for $\{3P_1, diamond\}$-free graphs. Furthermore, under TAR/TJ, it is always possible to reconfigure one separator into another.
   104	\end{theorem}
