     1	\section{Concluding Remarks}
     2	
     3	In this paper, we investigated the complexity of the reconfiguration of vertex separators under three commonly studied rules: token addition/removal (TAR), token jumping (TJ), and token sliding (TS).
     4	We showed that TAR and TJ are equivalent in the sense that, for every TJ-instance, there is a corresponding TAR-instance, and for every TAR instance, it is either trivially a negative instance, or we can find an equivalent TJ-instance.
     5	We then proceeded to show that \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} is \NPc\ under TAR/TJ and \PSPACE-\Complete\ under TJ for a subclass of bipartite graphs, which in turn implies that, for general graphs, the three rules are not equivalent to each other unless $\NP = \PSPACE$.
     6	On the positive side, we showed that, for every tame graph class, i.e. with a polynomially bounded number of minimal separators, under TAR/TJ, the reconfiguration problem can be solved in polynomial time; our final results explored classes where this assumption did not hold, namely $\{3P_1, diamond\}$-free -- for which we provide a novel characterization -- and series-parallel graphs, for which we actually prove that it is \textit{always} possible to reconfigure under TAR/TJ.
     7	
     8	In terms of future work under TAR/TJ, a natural investigation into the complexity of the problem for different non-tame graph classes is highly desired, particularly because the two examples we have of polynomially solvable cases are trivial in the sense that the answer is always positive, and computing the reconfiguration sequences is achievable by the algorithms we describe in our proofs.
     9	Perhaps there is no gray area and, for a non-tame class, the reconfiguration of vertex separators is either always possible, or answering this query is at least \NPH.
    10	We are specially interested in settling these questions for the two other classes that \citeauthor{Milanic}~\cite{Milanic} showed to be non-tame: $\{claw, K_4, C_4, diamond\}$-free and $\{K_3, C_4\}$-free graphs.
    11	Aside from these jumping rules, while the most obvious one is the study of \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} under TS, it also appears to be the most challenging, even on restricted classes.
    12	Regardless, it would be quite interesting to find reasonable sufficient conditions or non trivial examples where the reconfiguration problem becomes no harder than \NPc\ under TS.
    13	A related but more particular specific set of queries we would like to answer are about the parameterized complexity of \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration}; for instance, the tight relationship between treewidth and separators may yield useful tools to generalize our result on series parallel graphs to graphs of bounded treewidth.
    14	
    15	\begin{comment}
    16	It is also noteworthy that the connection between TS and TJ is quite loose.
    17	To generalize both rules, we can frame the problem as follows.
    18	Given a graph $G$ and a reconfiguration rule $\mathfrak{R}$ that preserves the cardinality of the token set, we can define the \textit{sliding graph} $G_\mathfrak{R} = (V(G), F)$ such that, between two states $A, B$ with $a \in A \setminus B$ and $b \in B \setminus A$, it must hold that $ab \in F$.
    19	Under this point view, if $\mathfrak{R} =$ TS, we have $G_{TS} = G$ and, if $\mathfrak{R} =$ TJ, $G_{TJ}$ is the complete graph.
    20	\end{comment}

