     1	\subsubsection{Series Parallel}
     2	
     3	The goal of this section is to show that, under TJ, it is always possible to reconfigure two $s,t$ separators in polynomial time.
     4	\textit{Series-parallel} graphs have multiple characterizations, such as being exactly the graphs of treewidth 2~\cite{2_trees}.
     5	One that is particularly useful for us is the recursive definition of \citeauthor{series_parallel}~\cite{series_parallel}, which we adapt below.
     6	
     7	\begin{definition}
     8	    Let $G$ be a multigraph.
     9	    \begin{enumerate}
    10	        \item If $|V(G)| = 1$ and $G$ has a loop, $G$ is a series-parallel graph;
    11	        \item If $G$ is series-parallel, the graph $H$ obtained by subdividing an edge of $G$ is series-parallel.
    12	        \item If $G$ is series-parallel, the graph $H$ obtained by removing one edge $e = uv$ of $G$ and replacing by two edges $f = g = e$ is series parallel.
    13	    \end{enumerate}
    14	    Operation 2 is known as the \textit{series} operation (or S), and operation 3 as the \textit{parallel} operation (or P).
    15	    $G$ is series-parallel if and only if its 2-connected components can be obtained from a loop by repeatedly applying operations S and P.
    16	    Observation~\ref{obs:sp_nontame} follows directly from this recursive definition.
    17	\end{definition}
    18	
    19	\begin{observation}
    20	    \label{obs:sp_nontame}
    21	    The class of series-parallel graphs is not tame.
    22	\end{observation}
    23	
    24	Note that, if we are concerned with 2-connected graphs, the first operation that acts on the loop must be a series operation, otherwise $G$ would have a cut vertex; since this is the case, we may, equivalently, assume that, instead of a loop, our initial graph is an edge and the first operation must be of type $P$.
    25	Let $\angled{G_1 , G_2, \dots, G_k}$ be a sequence of graphs such that $G_1$ is an edge, $G_k = G$ and for all $i \in [k-1]$, there is some edge of $G_i$ upon which we apply either operation $S$ or $P$ to obtain $G_{i+1}$, and let $\theta = \angled{(\phi_1, e_1), (\phi_2, e_2), \dots, (\phi_{k-1}, e_{k-1})}$ be the corresponding sequence of $S,P$ operations; i.e. $\phi_i \in \{S,P\}$ and $e_i \in E(G_i)$ but $e_i \notin E(G_j)$ for any $j > i$.
    26	By our definition, $(\phi_1, e_1) = (P, e_1)$.
    27	
    28	We say that edge $e \in G_j$ is a \textit{descendant} of edge $e_i \notin \bigcup_{\ell \geq j} E(G_\ell)$ if $e_j$ is the result of $\phi_i$ applied to $e_i$ or if it is the result of $\phi_r$, $r < j$ applied to some descendant $e_r$ of $e_i$.
    29	Two edges $e, f$ are \textit{unrelated} if $e$ is not a descendant of $f$ and vice-versa.
    30	In particular, every edge of $G$ is a descendant of the original loop and, if $e$ is a descendant of $e_j$ and of $e_\ell$, either $e_j$ is a descendant of $e_\ell$ or vice-versa.
    31	This relationship can be seen as a full binary rooted tree $\mathcal{T}$, where each internal vertex $t_i$ corresponds to some $(\phi_i, e_i)$, the leaves are precisely the edges of $G$, and $t \in V(\mathcal{T})$ is a descendant of $t_i$ in the tree if and only if the edge associated to $t$ is a descendant of $e_i$.
    32	Moreover, the root of the tree is given by $(\phi_1, e_1)$ with $\phi_1 = P$.
    33	We call $\mathcal{T}$ a \textit{PS-tree} of $G$ and present an example of one in Figure~\ref{fig:ps_tree}.
