   219	        \item[Sequential Pair] Suppose without loss of generality that $\supp(t) = e_i = sa$, define $V(s,t)$ as the vertices $z_j$ that have an $st$-edge as support,
   220	        and let $f_j = sz_j$ and $g_j = z_jt$ be the edges created by $(S, e_j) \in V(\mathcal{T})$.
   221	        We show that $\{a\} \cup V(s,t)$ is an $st$-separator of minimum size by induction on $|V(s,t)|$.
   222	        For the base case of $V(s,t) = \{z_j\}$, no piece of $f_j$ is adjacent to a piece of $g_j$, so any $s - t$ path that uses a piece of either of these edges must pass through $z_j$; similarly, any path that does not use a piece of $st$ must pass through $a$.
   223	        By Menger's Theorem, $\{z_j, a\}$ is a minimum $st$-separator.
   224	        For the general case, take any $z_j \in V(s,t)$, let $G'$ be the induced subgraph of $G$ obtained by the removal of all pieces of $\supp(z_j)$, and note that $G'$ is a series parallel graph with one fewer operation $P$ applied to an edge $st$.
   225	        By induction, $V(s,t) \setminus \{z_j\} \cup \{a\}$ is an $st$-separator of $G'$.
   226	        Since the only $s-t$ paths on $G$ not on $G'$ are those that use exclusively pieces of $\supp(z_j)$ and must pass through $z_j$, we have that $V(s,t) \cup \{a\}$ is an $st$-separator.
   227	        Note that $|V(s,t)| = \varepsilon(s,t) + 1$ since each operation $P$ increases $\varepsilon(s,t)$ by one and the first $st$-edge is the result of an $S$ operation.
   228	        As such, it holds that the size of a minimum $s-t$ separator is $|\{a\} \cup V(s,t)| = \varepsilon(s,t) + 2$.
