    64	\begin{proof}
    65	    For the forward direction, suppose $G$ has a universal vertex $v$; since $G$ is not a clique, $\diam(G) = 2$, and let $\{u,v,w\}$ be an induced $P_3$ of $G$.
    66	    Since $G$ is $3P_1$-free, any $z \in V(G)$ is either in $N(u)$ or $N(v)$ but, since $G$ is also diamond-free and $v$ is universal, $z$ is not in both, otherwise $\{u,v,w,z\}$ is a diamond; moreover, if there are two non-adjacent vertices $a,b \in N(u)$, $\{a,u,v,b\}$ is an induced diamond, so $N[u]$ and $N[w]$ are cliques.1 Thus, $G$ satisfies condition (i) with $v$ as a cut vertex.
    67	    
    68	    If $G$ does not have a universal vertex, let $u,w \in V(G)$ be a pair of non-adjacent vertices and note that $N(u) \cup N(w) \cup \{u,w\} = V(G)$, otherwise $G$ would not be $3P_1$-free.
    69	    Furthermore, there are no two adjacent vertices in $N(u) \cap N(w)$, otherwise we would have a diamond, $N(u) \setminus N(w)$ is a clique, otherwise $G\left[\{w\} \cup N(u) \setminus N(w)\right]$ would contain $3P_1$ as an induced subgraph, and no vertex in  $N(u) \setminus N(w)$ is adjacent to more than one vertex in $N(w)$, otherwise we would have an induced diamond.
    70	    We branch our analysis on the size of $N(u) \cap N(w)$.
    71	    \begin{enumerate}
    72	        \item If $N(u) \cap N(w) = \emptyset$, no vertex of $N(u)$ has more than one neighbor in $N(w)$, so the edge between $N(u)$ and $N(w)$ form a matching.
    73	        
    74	        \item Suppose that $\{v\} = N(u) \cap N(w)$. 
    75	        If there is some $a \in N(u)$ that $v$ is not adjacent to, $v$ is not adjacent to \textit{any} vertex $b \in N(u)$, otherwise we would have $\{a, u, b, v\}$ as an induced diamond of $G$.
    76	        In turn, $v$ is either adjacent the entirety of $N(u)$ or to no vertex of $N(u)$ (the same holds with respect to $N(w)$).
    77	        However, since $v$ is not universal and w.l.o.g, it is not adjacent to $N(u)$.
    78	        We have two subcases.
    79	        \begin{enumerate}
    80	            \item If $v$ is not adjacent to $N(w)$, each vertex in $N(w)$ must be adjacent to every vertex in $N(u)$, otherwise we would have an induced $3P_1$; however, if $\max\left\{|N(u)|, |N(w)|\right\} \geq 2$, we have an induced diamond, so $G$ must be a $C_5$, a contradiction to our hypothesis.
    81	            \item If $v$ is adjacent to $N(w)$ we are done: $V(G)$ is partitioned into the cliques $N[u], N[w]$ and the edges between them form a matching.
    82	        \end{enumerate}
    83	        \item Finally, if $\{v,v'\} = N(u) \cap N(w)$, we cannot have both $v,v'$ non-adjacent to the same vertex $a \in N(u) \setminus N(w)$, otherwise $\{a,v,v'\}$ form an induced $3P_1$ of $G$, nor have both of them adjacent to $a$, otherwise $\{v, a, u, v'\}$ would form an induced diamond; as such, suppose $v$ is adjacent to every vertex in $N(u) \setminus \{v, v'\}$.
    84	        We again have to branch in two subcases.
    85	        \begin{itemize}
    86	            \item If $v$ is not adjacent to the vertices in $N(w) \setminus \{v,v'\}$, $v'$ must be adjacent to them.
    87	            Therefore, we can partition $G$ into the cliques $(N[u] \setminus v'), (N[w]  \setminus v)$ and the edges between the cliques form a matching, otherwise we would have an induced diamond.
    88	            \item If $v$ is adjacent to $N(w) \setminus \{v, v'\}$, then at least one of $N(w) \setminus \{v, v'\}$, $N(u) \setminus \{v, v'\}$ is empty, otherwise we have either a copy of $3P_1$ that includes $v'$ or an induced diamond $\{u,v,a,b\}$, where $a \in N(u)$ and $b \in N(w)$.
    89	        \end{itemize}
    90	    \end{enumerate}
    91	    
    92	    It is straightforward to verify that $\diam(G) \leq 3$.
    93	    The converse follows directly and we omit it for brevity.
    94	\end{proof}
    95	
    96	The proof of the following theorem relies on the fact that the only minimal separators are either the universal cut vertex or we must choose, for each edge between the cliques, exactly one of its endpoints.
    97	In the first case, the answer for \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} under TAR/TJ is always \YES, while under TS it suffices to check whether the number of tokens on each clique is equal in the initial and final separators; for an example of this case we refer to Figure~\ref{fig:3p1_diamond_case_1}.
    98	For the latter, the case under TAR/TJ boils down to the same analysis.
    99	For TS however, things require a bit more of work: if $u,v$ are the vertices we want to separate and every edge between $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ has an endpoint in $\{u,v\}$, then the analysis is also equivalent to the previous case; if, on the other hand, there is at least one edge that has neither $u$ nor $v$ as an endpoint, we can freely move tokens between $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ through it.
   100	For an example of each of theses cases, we point to Figure~\ref{fig:3p1_diamond_case_2}.
   101	
   102	\begin{theorem}
   103	    Under TAR/TJ and TS, \pname{Vertex Separator Reconfiguration} can be solved in polynomial time for $\{3P_1, diamond\}$-free graphs. Furthermore, under TAR/TJ, it is always possible to reconfigure one separator into another.
   104	\end{theorem}
   105	
