   316	We refer to the separators described in the proof of our previous theorem as the \textit{canonical $st$-separators}, and denote them by $M(s,t)$.
   317	
   318	From now on, our strategy for reconfiguring an $s-t$ separator $A$ into another $s-t$ separator $B$ will consist in reconfiguring them into some intermediate separator containing $M(s,t)$. 
   319	Note that this may be far from trivial; for instance, when $s,t \in V(G)$ form a parallel pair, it may require quite a bit of work to reconfigure a separator to contain $M(s,t)$, as it may not be possible to directly exchange a token in $A$ for a token in $M(s,t)$, as shown by the example in Figure~\ref{fig:parallel_pair}.
